{
  "comment": "What a `memphis_self_modify` call carries. The registry declares five arguments — `intent`, `files`, `changes`, and the optional `plan_id`/`step_idx` pair — and every arm turns a call into the SAME canonical payload: the fields in the registry's order, the changes keyed in the order of `files`, the optional plan reference present only when it was given. TypeScript (src/infra/runtime/evolve-request.ts, `canonicalEvolveRequest`) and Rust (crates/memphis-operator/src/chat_self_modify.rs, `canonical_self_modify_request`) are mirrors of one rule, and the runners are tests/unit/self-modify-contract.test.ts and crates/memphis-operator/tests/self_modify_contract.rs. WHY: this tool had THREE implementations that disagreed — the MCP server read the registry's fields, the gateway's own executor normalised them first and filtered junk out of `files` instead of refusing it, and the native arm accepted `{path, content, mode}` and wrote ONE file where the registry means 'apply changes to many files'. One name, two operations. The digests below are the proof that the arms agree: the same call must produce the same bytes, so the operator sees one digest everywhere. They are computed by a third implementation (scripts/regenerate-self-modify-contract-fixture.mts), so neither arm can satisfy the table by agreeing with itself. The `expand` field grows one file's content, so the one-MiB rule is in the table without putting a megabyte into the repository.",
